module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import MathlibExt.Geometry.Euclidean.ApollonianCirclePacking

/-!
# Apollonian circle packings: the complex Descartes theorem and circle counting

An Apollonian circle packing is built from four mutually tangent circles by repeatedly replacing
one circle of a Descartes configuration by the other circle tangent to the remaining three
(Lagarias, Mallows and Wilks, *Beyond the Descartes circle theorem*, Amer. Math. Monthly 109
(2002), arXiv:math/0101066v1, Section 2; H. Oh, *Apollonian circle packings: dynamics and number
theory*, Japan. J. Math. 9 (2014), arXiv:1312.1383v2, Section 1, after Theorem 1.1).

This file records, as wanted statements:

* the complex Descartes theorem of Lagarias, Mallows and Wilks (their Theorem 2.1), the relation
  `(∑ kᵢ zᵢ)² = 2 ∑ (kᵢ zᵢ)²` between the signed curvatures `kᵢ` and the centers `zᵢ ∈ ℂ`;
* the circle-counting theorem of Kontorovich and Oh (*Apollonian circle packings and closed
  horospheres on hyperbolic 3-manifolds*, J. Amer. Math. Soc. 24 (2011), arXiv:0811.2236v5,
  Theorem 1.1): in a bounded Apollonian packing the number of circles of curvature at most `T` is
  asymptotic to `c · T^α`, where `α` is the Hausdorff dimension of the residual set.

The circle-level definitions (circles of positive radius, tangency as a one-point intersection,
Descartes configurations, the packing generated by swaps, its residual set and its counting
function) are in `MathlibExt.Geometry.Euclidean.ApollonianCirclePacking`.
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.Geometry.ApollonianCirclePackingWanted

open Filter Topology MathlibExt.Geometry.Euclidean.ApollonianCirclePacking

/-- **Complex Descartes theorem.** Let four circles in the plane `ℂ` have centers `cᵢ` and radii
`rᵢ > 0`, and signed curvatures `kᵢ`, in either configuration of Descartes' circle theorem: (a)
pairwise externally tangent, `|cᵢ - cⱼ| = rᵢ + rⱼ`, with `kᵢ = 1/rᵢ`; or (b) one circle `e`
internally tangent to the three others, `|c_e - cⱼ| = r_e - rⱼ`, which are pairwise externally
tangent, with `k_e = -1/r_e` and `kⱼ = 1/rⱼ` for `j ≠ e`. Then the products `kᵢ cᵢ` of curvature
and center satisfy the same relation as the curvatures,
`(∑ kᵢ cᵢ)² = 2 ∑ (kᵢ cᵢ)²` in `ℂ` (Lagarias, Mallows and Wilks, *Beyond the Descartes circle
theorem*, arXiv:math/0101066v1, Theorem 2.1, stated there for all Descartes configurations,
lines included). -/
theorem_wanted complex_descartes_theorem (c : Fin 4 → ℂ) (r k : Fin 4 → ℝ)
    (hr : ∀ i, 0 < r i)
    (htan : ((∀ i j, i ≠ j → dist (c i) (c j) = r i + r j) ∧ ∀ i, k i = 1 / r i) ∨
      ∃ e : Fin 4, k e = -1 / r e ∧ (∀ j, j ≠ e → k j = 1 / r j) ∧
        (∀ j, j ≠ e → dist (c e) (c j) = r e - r j) ∧
        ∀ i j, i ≠ e → j ≠ e → i ≠ j → dist (c i) (c j) = r i + r j) :
    (∑ i, (k i : ℂ) * c i) ^ 2 = 2 * ∑ i, ((k i : ℂ) * c i) ^ 2

/-- **Kontorovich–Oh circle-counting theorem.** Let `P` be a bounded Apollonian circle packing,
presented by a Descartes configuration `C₀` containing its bounding circle `C₀ e` (the other three
circles are internally tangent to it), and let `α` be the Hausdorff dimension of the residual set
of `P`. Then there is `c > 0` with `N_P(T) ∼ c · T^α` as `T → ∞`, that is,
`N_P(T) / T^α → c` (A. Kontorovich and H. Oh, *Apollonian circle packings and closed horospheres
on hyperbolic 3-manifolds*, J. Amer. Math. Soc. 24 (2011), arXiv:0811.2236v5, Theorem 1.1, bounded
case; restated as H. Oh, arXiv:1312.1383v2, Theorem 1.5). Every bounded packing has such a
presentation, for instance by its root quadruple, whose negative entry is the bounding circle
(Kontorovich and Oh, Definition 2.2); numerically
`α ≈ 1.30568` (McMullen). -/
theorem_wanted kontorovich_oh_circle_count (C₀ : Fin 4 → PlaneCircle)
    (hC₀ : IsDescartesConfiguration C₀) (e : Fin 4)
    (he : ∀ j, j ≠ e → dist (C₀ e).center (C₀ j).center = (C₀ e).radius - (C₀ j).radius) :
    ∃ c : ℝ, 0 < c ∧
      Tendsto (fun T : ℝ => (circleCount C₀ T : ℝ) / T ^ (dimH (residualSet C₀)).toReal)
        atTop (𝓝 c)

end MathlibExt.Geometry.ApollonianCirclePackingWanted
